# Rule: user-facing text goes through Lingui

Kangentic's UI is translated with Lingui 6 (GitHub #489). English stays inline in the source, and a
message's ID is a hash of its English, so rewording a string makes it new to every translation. A
production renderer keeps only each message's ID. Text that never reached a catalog therefore shows
as a raw ID like `z3fd2`, and text never wrapped in a macro shows English in every language. The
setup, the catalog layout and the decision record are in
[docs/architecture/i18n.md](../../docs/architecture/i18n.md).

## The rule

- **Macro by file type.** A `.tsx` file uses `<Trans>`, `<Plural>` and `useLingui()`'s `t` from
  `@lingui/react/macro`. Every `.ts` module, renderer ones included, uses `msg` from
  `@lingui/core/runtime-macro` with `i18n._(...)` or `translateText(...)`. Main bundles with plain
  esbuild, and Playwright and the capture rig load `.ts` modules outside Vite. A build-time macro
  throws in all three places.
- **Translate where it renders, never at module load.** A module-level list holds `msg`
  descriptors, resolved with `translateText` (`src/renderer/i18n/translate.ts`) or `settingProps`
  where they render. Type a converted list's field `MessageDescriptor`, not `LocalizedText`, so a new
  entry cannot be a plain string. Keep `LocalizedText` only where a plain string is a real value
  (a protocol token, an example URL). A `t` at module scope runs before the session's catalog loads, so
  it stays English for good.
- **Text with two audiences resolves at each reader.** The renderer uses `translateText`; main's
  agent text, stored run reasons and persisted names use `englishText`. A `${descriptor}` in a
  template literal prints `[object Object]` and no compiler catches it. A refusal main throws stays
  English and comes paired with a message for the UI (`WORKFLOW_REFUSALS` and
  `WORKFLOW_REFUSAL_MESSAGES`), because the renderer matches the thrown text exactly.
- **Name placeholders.** Pull a member expression into a local first, so the translator sees
  `{projectName}`, not `{0}`. The runtime macro takes them labeled, `${{ name }}` and
  `plural({ count }, ...)`: outside the transform (main) a raw `${count}` or `plural(count, ...)`
  throws.
- **Plurals are ICU plurals**, `<Plural value={count} one="# task" other="# tasks" />` or
  `plural(...)`, never `count === 1 ? '' : 's'`.
- **Give ambiguous short strings a `context`** (`msg({ message: 'Done', context: 'column name' })`).
  "Done", "Merge" and "Open" read differently in different places, and one English string is one ID.
  A string drawn in more than one area with different translations needs a context too. The renderer
  merges a locale's areas into one map, and an untranslated copy can replace a translation
  (`tests/unit/i18n-boundaries.test.ts` fails on that). The extractor reads only a literal context.
- **Quote literal braces.** ICU treats `{` as a placeholder, so literal template keywords are quoted:
  `'{{task_xml}}'` renders `{{task_xml}}`.
- **The preload imports nothing that holds `msg`.** The runtime macro needs `node:crypto`, which a
  sandboxed preload cannot load. Put a constant the preload needs in its own module, as
  `src/shared/pop-out-arg.ts` does.
- **Text an agent reads stays English.** MCP tool output, agent prompts, reasons stored for
  `kangentic_get_automation_runs`, and template keyword names resolve a descriptor with `englishText`
  (`src/shared/i18n/english.ts`), never with the UI translator and never with `descriptor.message`.
- **Each area owns one PO file per locale** (`src/locales/<locale>/<area>.po`). An area is a folder
  under `src/renderer/components/`, or one of `src-renderer`, `src-shared` and `src-main`
  (lingui.config.ts). Run `npm run i18n:extract` after adding, changing or removing a message, and
  commit `src/locales` with it. Commit only your own area's catalogs.
- **A machine translation is marked `fuzzy` until reviewed.** `npm run i18n:translate` drafts empty
  entries and marks each one. Clear the flag only after reviewing the entry, and never hand-mark a
  reviewed entry `fuzzy`, since `--redraft` rewrites marked entries
  ([docs/contributing/translations.md](../../docs/contributing/translations.md)).
- **A language ships deliberately.** `SHIPPED_LOCALES` (`src/shared/i18n/locales.ts`) lists the
  languages the picker offers and the first-launch suggestion may offer. Add one only once its
  catalogs are translated, the six first-launch strings (`LANGUAGE_PROMPT_MESSAGES`) included, and
  give it a translated README with `npm run i18n:translate-readme -- --locales <code>`.

## Enforcement (self-maintaining)

- **Test:** `tests/unit/i18n-hardcoded-text.test.ts` parses every renderer `.tsx` and fails on JSX
  text, a literal child, or a literal UI prop (label, placeholder, aria-label, a dialog's title...)
  outside a macro, and on a module-scope `t`. Files not yet extracted are allowlisted by catalog area
  in `tests/unit/helpers/i18n-unconverted-files.ts`, and a listed file with nothing left fails, so the
  list only shrinks. Text that must stay as written takes `{/* i18n-ok: <reason> */}`. A second scan
  in the same file reads every renderer `.ts` and `.tsx` for a literal `title`, `message` or
  `action.label` handed to `addToast(...)` or `updateToast(id, ...)`, which the JSX scan cannot see;
  its own shrink-only list is `tests/unit/helpers/i18n-unconverted-toast-files.ts`. It reads only
  an object literal written in the call, so a toast raised through an alias of `addToast`, a toast
  object built in a variable first, and a shorthand field (`{ title }`) are out of its reach.
- **Test:** `tests/unit/i18n-boundaries.test.ts` fails when a file that declares messages is in no
  catalog or in two, when two catalogs share a file, when a `.ts` module imports a build-time macro,
  when MCP or agent command code imports the UI translator, when main's esbuild bundle stops
  resolving a shared list's descriptors, when the preload bundle reaches `@lingui`, when a
  runtime-macro placeholder or `plural`/`select` argument is not labeled, when a shipped language
  lacks the first-launch strings, and
  when one message reads one way in one area and another way, or untranslated, in another. The
  renderer merges a locale's areas and the last one wins, so a correction to one copy of a message
  does not show until every copy matches. `npm run i18n:translate` copies a message another area
  already translates instead of drafting it again.
- **Test:** `tests/unit/readme-translations.test.ts` fails when a shipped language has no
  `i18n/README.<locale>.md`, a translated README's language is not shipped or its file sits at the
  root, and when a README's switcher does not list exactly `SHIPPED_LOCALES` in order. `/release`
  refreshes stale translations ([docs/contributing/translations.md](../../docs/contributing/translations.md)).
- **Gate (CI and release):** `npm run i18n:check` (`scripts/i18n-catalogs.js`) runs `lingui check
  sync`, which fails exactly when `lingui extract` would change a file, and `lingui check missing` for
  English. CI's checks job runs it as its own step, and `scripts/build.js` (the release build) and
  `scripts/build-demo.js` (the demo deploy) run it before building, failing loudly with the fix.
- **Test:** `tests/unit/vite-refresh-hook-signatures.test.ts` fails when a renderer Vite config or
  `vitest.config.mts` drops the Lingui plugin or the catalog hot update.
- **Review:** `/code-review` checks message quality the scans cannot: a `context` where the same
  English means two things, a placeholder a translator can read, a sentence not split across messages.

## Scope

User-facing text in the renderer, the shared lists main and the renderer both import, and main's own
menus, dialogs and notifications. What stays English (agent and terminal output, MCP text, seeded
column names, release notes, Monaco's and Chromium's own strings) is listed in the docs concept.
